# Local secrets and environment (never commit)
.env
.env.local
.env.*
!.env.example

# Keys and certificates
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
secrets/

# Local databases that may contain real or test PII
*.db
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
data/*
# ...except the built-in IP->country table, which is the whole point of being
# distributable: it is public RIR allocation data with no PII, it is what makes
# flags work with nothing installed, and a deployment cannot build without it.
# Regenerate with tools/gen_ip_country.py; the RIR sources it downloads stay
# ignored by the line above.
!data/ip_country.bin
# The chat filter word list is source (include_str! at build), not data.
!data/chat_filter_words.txt
# The automatch rulesets are SOURCE, not per-deployment config, and both files
# are tracked.
#
# This reverses an earlier call. `automatch_rulesets.toml` was ignored on the
# grounds that a committed one "would turn automatch on for everybody who
# cloned this" -- true, but the thing that matters more is that a player gets
# the SAME match on every server, and queue settings that each operator writes
# by hand do not converge on their own. So the canonical set is maintained
# here, ships in the release bundle, and a deployment that needs to diverge
# points AUTOMATCH_RULESETS_PATH at its own file rather than editing this one.
# The divergence then lives in that server's environment, where it is visible,
# instead of in a local edit the next bundle would silently revert.
!data/automatch_rulesets.toml
# The example stays too: it documents the format, and carries the PSX caps
# vocabulary that the shipping SNES entries do not show.
!data/automatch_rulesets.example.toml

# Rust
/target/
**/*.rs.bk

# IDE / OS
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
.DS_Store

# Logs
*.log
psx_last_run_report.json

# Deployment bundles produced by tools/bundle.sh
/dist/
